## The Numbers Don't Lie

This month (June 2026) saw 78 reported recalls, pushing this year's total to 577--a pace that might well outdo last year. The recalls spanned various classes, with 176 Class I recalls to date this year alone, emphasizing the severity of certain violations. And let's not overlook the 87 repeat offenders on our radar, a concerning indication of systemic issues within these brands.

## The Repeat Offenders

A notorious name on this list is Galaxy RG Corp, which contributed significantly to this month's recall tally. The company issued an alarming 10 recalls on June 17th related to undeclared synthetic food colorants in their line of sweet confections. Products like Marquesita de Chocolate y Vainilla and Cheesecake de Guava were all cited, proving that even the sweetest treats can leave a sour taste when safety measures are ignored.

## Class I Concern

On the other end of the severity spectrum, we see a Class I recall from Total Nutrition Inc for their dietary supplements potentially contaminated with Salmonella. Their Complete Green Superfood Capsules now come with more than just a serving of greens--if you're lucky, you might get a side of bacterial infection. Jokes aside, this is a serious issue that highlights the need for rigorous quality assurance, especially in products people consume for their health.

## The Need for Better Oversight

While the industry has made strides in tracing and recalling unsafe products, persistent issues point to a lack of preventive measures. The top offenders, including Whole Foods' parent company Albertsons Companies LLC and others, need to prioritize consumer safety over other interests.

## Conclusion

The recurring names in the recall logs beg the question: what are we missing? Is it more stringent inspections, better consumer awareness, or just accountability?

For now, the saga continues, and Shelf Check will be here documenting every twist and turn.
